    "q-118557": {
      "dom_id": "step-q118557",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "The population $y(t)$ of turtles on a certain island follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1.5y-0.001{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=500,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in years, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible population of turtles?",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible population. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157279/assets/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =1.5y-0.001{y}^{2} \\\\  & =0.001y(1\\,500-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=1\\,500.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $1\\,500,$ and we conclude that the number of turtles cannot exceed $1\\,500.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
