    "q-353476": {
      "dom_id": "step-q353476",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "The number $y(t)$ of people in a closed community who have been infected with a virus follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2400y-3{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=12,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, in days, since the observations began. According to this model, what is the maximum possible number of infected people?\n\nThe maximum possible number of infected people is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity. \n\nThe carrying capacity $a$ represents the maximum possible number of people who will be infected. A diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157279/assets/d8133f1c4203903bac606bbae61c73717e2ba2dc15a97a77f48eaaeaf4736d06.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2400y-3{y}^{2} \\\\  & =3y(800-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=800.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $800,$ and we conclude that the maximum possible number of infected people is $800.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
