    "q-15486": {
      "dom_id": "step-q15486",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "The population $P(t)$ of squirrels in a certain forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,5P(6\\,000-P),\\,P(0)=1\\,000\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the squirrel population growing at the fastest possible rate?",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157279/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFrom our equation, we see that $a=6\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $6\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\,000=3\\,000.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
