    "q-51379": {
      "dom_id": "step-q51379",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "The population $P(t)$of rabbits in a particular forest follows the logistic growth model\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=4P-0.002{P}^{2},\\,P(0)=100,\n$$\n\nwhere $t$ is the time, measured in years. For which value of $P$ is the rabbit population growing at the fastest possible rate?",
      "worked_solution": "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nFor a logistic growth model, the fastest rate of growth occurs when $P=\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14157279/assets/9d8f5340bba4d3c407e43f2bc64a4aa2ea986be89b8024baaa4e8ae65e96e5d5.png)\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the right-hand side of the given equation so that it matches the form above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dP}{dt} & =4P-0.002{P}^{2} \\\\  & =0.002P(2\\,000-P)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=2\\,000.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $2\\,000,$ and $P$ grows at its fastest possible rate when \n\n$$\nP=\\frac{1}{2}a=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\,000=1\\,000.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
